---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="Methodology">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p>
    <strong>The data is real. The relationship is not.</strong> Here's exactly how a chart on
    this site gets made.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Fetch</h2>
  <p>
    Every dataset is pulled by a script directly from the source's own public API — never typed
    in by hand. Each fetch is recorded with a timestamp, the exact request URL used, and a
    provenance tag, so it's traceable back to a real API response.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Pair</h2>
  <p>
    Two datasets are only paired if they come from unrelated domains, come from different
    source organizations, share the same frequency (no mixing daily with monthly), and overlap
    in time by enough points to be worth showing. Domain pairs that plausibly do influence each
    other — weather and air quality, economy and agriculture — are blocked outright, regardless
    of what the numbers show.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Normalize</h2>
  <p>
    Both series are indexed to 100 at the first date they share, so two very differently scaled
    numbers (a population count and a temperature reading, say) can sit on one chart. A series
    that's zero or negative at that starting point, or crosses zero anywhere in the shared
    window, can't be indexed this way without distorting its shape — so that pairing is rejected
    outright rather than patched with an offset or a different start date.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Present</h2>
  <p>
    Every chart ships with a fake-serious "insight," an immediate reveal explaining why it
    doesn't hold up, and a source panel showing both datasets' organizations, licenses, date
    ranges, units, and the exact baseline values used to compute the index. None of these four
    pieces appears without the others.
  </p>

  <p class="disclaimer-line">
    Shared movement does not imply relationship, influence, or causation.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .disclaimer-line {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding-top: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
</style>
